As rails user:
* cd /home/rails
* mkdir -p apps/brick_city_depot
* chown -R rails:rails /home/rails/apps

* echo "alias bcd='cd /home/rails/apps/brick_city_depot/current'" >> ~/.bashrc
* source ~/.bashrc
* echo "gem: --no-document" > ~/.gemrc

* cd /home/rails/apps/brick_city_depot
* git clone https://github.com/jasonlyles/bcd.git
* mv bcd current

As root user:
* sudo apt update
* sudo apt install git curl libssl-dev libreadline-dev zlib1g-dev autoconf bison build-essential libyaml-dev libreadline-dev libncurses5-dev libffi-dev libgdbm-dev
ADD SOME SWAP MEMORY SO WE CAN BUNDLE INSTALL:
* sudo fallocate -l 1G /swapfile
* sudo chmod 600 /swapfile
* sudo mkswap /swapfile
* sudo swapon /swapfile

SETTING UP THE DB:
* exit (to get to the root user)
* upgrading postgres (if necessary):
  * sudo sh -c 'echo "deb http://apt.postgresql.org/pub/repos/apt $(lsb_release -cs)-pgdg main" > /etc/apt/sources.list.d/pgdg.list'
  * wget -qO- https://www.postgresql.org/media/keys/ACCC4CF8.asc | sudo tee /etc/apt/trusted.gpg.d/pgdg.asc &>/dev/null
  * sudo apt update
  * sudo apt install postgresql
  * psql --version (to check we're on the new version)
* sudo -u postgres psql
* (in psql) create database brickcity_production; (if the database is being rebuilt)
* In preparation for pg_restore-ing from the Heroku dump:
  * \c brickcity_production;
  * CREATE SCHEMA IF NOT EXISTS heroku_ext;
  * ALTER database brickcity_production SET search_path TO heroku_ext,public;
* \q (to exit out of psql)


* vim /etc/systemd/system/rails.service
  * Edit the WorkingDirectory and ExecStart directives
  * Add the following directive:
    [Service]
    Environment="PATH=/home/rails/.rbenv/shims:/home/rails/.rbenv/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin"
* systemctl daemon-reload
* rvm implode (having rvm in addition to rbenv causes some interference)
* Remove `source...` rvm line from ~/.bashrc

* vim /etc/sudoers.d/rails
  Add the following:
  rails ALL=NOPASSWD: /bin/systemctl status rails, /bin/systemctl restart sidekiq, /bin/systemctl status sidekiq, /bin/systemctl restart puma, /bin/systemctl status puma, /bin/systemctl restart rails, /bin/systemctl start rails
* sudo chmod 440 /etc/sudoers.d/rails

SET UP REDIS
Helpful: https://www.digitalocean.com/community/tutorials/how-to-install-and-secure-redis-on-ubuntu-22-04 and
           https://www.digitalocean.com/community/tutorials/how-to-install-and-configure-redis-on-ubuntu-16-04

* curl -fsSL https://packages.redis.io/gpg | sudo gpg --dearmor -o /usr/share/keyrings/redis-archive-keyring.gpg
* sudo chmod 644 /usr/share/keyrings/redis-archive-keyring.gpg
* echo "deb [signed-by=/usr/share/keyrings/redis-archive-keyring.gpg] https://packages.redis.io/deb $(lsb_release -cs) main" | sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/redis.list
* sudo apt-get update
* sudo apt-get install redis

* sudo vim /etc/redis/redis.conf
  Inside the file, find the supervised directive. Set it to: systemd
  Uncomment this line: bind 127.0.0.1 ::1
* sudo systemctl restart redis.service

As rails user:
* curl -fsSL https://github.com/rbenv/rbenv-installer/raw/HEAD/bin/rbenv-installer | bash
* source ~/.bashrc
* rbenv install 3.3.6
* bcd
* bundle config set without development test
* bundle
* echo "<add production key here>" > config/credentials/production.key
* echo "<add production key here>" > config/master.key
* RAILS_ENV=production bundle exec rails db:migrate
* RAILS_ENV=production bundle exec rails db:seed (if necessary)

* RAILS_ENV=production bundle exec rake assets:precompile
* sudo systemctl start rails

THINGS TO DO AFTER THE APP IS RUNNING:

logrotate: https://gorails.com/guides/rotating-rails-production-logs-with-logrotate
* /etc/logrotate.conf
* Add the following at the bottom of the file:
  /home/rails/apps/brick_city_depot/current/log/*.log {
    daily
    missingok
    rotate 7
    compress
    delaycompress
    notifempty
    copytruncate
  }
* Run `sudo /usr/sbin/logrotate -f /etc/logrotate.conf` a couple of times to make sure
  it's rotating. Will only work if there is an existing log file. Hit the server in
  between requests to make sure it's also compressing properly.

DATABASE BACKUPS:
* sudo apt install awscli
* mkdir ~/.aws
* vim ~/.aws/credentials (copy in aws creds from my local machine, and be sure to include the [default] header, and the us-east-1 region)
* Make sure the following vars are set in .bashrc:
  * export AWS_REGION=us-east-1
  * export S3_BUCKET_NAME=lyles-pg-database-backups
  * export DATABASE_NAME=brickcity_production
  * export RAILS_SERVE_STATIC_FILES=enabled
  * export RAILS_LOG_TO_STDOUT=enabled
* source ~/.bashrc
* cd ~
* vim pg_backup.sh
* copy in file from here: server/pg_backup.sh
* chmod +x pg_backup.sh
* Test by running `./pg_backup.sh` (Confirm in S3 there's an actual backup in there.)
Set up systemd timer to run the job
* exit (to get to the root user)
* cd /etc/systemd/system
* vim pg-backup.service
  * paste in the contents of server/pg-backup.service
* systemctl start pg-backup.service
* systemctl status pg-backup.service (to confirm it ran properly)
* vim pg-backup.timer
  * paste in contents of server/pg-backup.timer
* systemctl daemon-reload
* systemctl restart pg-backup.service
* systemctl restart pg-backup.timer

TO RESTORE FROM A BACKUP:
* Download the backup from S3
* scp it to the server
* unzip it
* psql -d newdb -f db.sql (where newdb is the name of the new database, and db.sql is the unzipped dump file)

COPY IN LATEST FROM HEROKU DB TO NEW DB:
On my machine, from bcd root:
  * heroku pg:backups:capture --app bcd-prod
  * heroku pg:backups:download --app bcd-prod
  * scp latest.dump root@143.244.144.159:~/
On server:
  * cd /root
  * pg_restore --verbose --clean --no-acl --no-owner -h localhost -U rails -d brickcity_production latest.dump
  * sudo su rails
  * bcd
  * RAILS_ENV=production bundle exec rails db:migrate
  * go into rails console and set maintenance_mode to true

SET UP SIDEKIQ:
* copy in file from server/sidekiq.service to /etc/systemd/system/sidekiq.service and start the service.
